= Implicature — Simple =

An '''implicature''' is something a speaker communicates without saying it directly.

If someone asks “Did every student pass?” and the answer is “Some students passed,” people may understand that not all of them passed.

But “some” does not logically mean “some but not all.” The extra message comes from how people normally communicate in that [[Context (linguistics)/Simple|context]].

This kind of extra meaning can often be canceled: “Some students passed—in fact, all of them did.”

Implicature is different from [[Presupposition/Simple|presupposition]], which usually treats information as backgrounded or already assumed.

